#{[17592186527123 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527123 #edb/ref 1069 "q-185123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527123 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527123 #edb/ref 1071 "$(-70)^{2}=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527123 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01e96138-a1bd-5aa7-aa98-7c5862390efd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527123 #edb/ref 1074 "To work out $(-70)^{2},$ we multiply $-70$ by itself:\n\n$$\n(-70)^{2}=(-70)\\cdot (-70)=4,900\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-169408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527137 #edb/ref 1071 "Anna makes two cakes. The first cake weighs $1,200\\,\\text{g},$ while the second cake weighs $140%$ of the weight of the first. What is the weight of the second cake?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21a2d2fe-461f-5c6a-9efc-076472c2d6af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527137 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the first cake, which is $1,200\\,\\text{g}.$ The percentage is $140%,$ and the part is the weight of the second cake.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140%=140÷100=1.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =140%\\times 1,200 \\\\  & =1.4\\times 1,200 \\\\  & =1,680\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $140%$ of $1,200$ is $1,680.$ So, the weight of the second cake is $1,680\\,\\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n140%=\\frac{140}{100}=\\frac{140÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =140%\\times 1,200 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\times 1,200 \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{1,200}{5} \\\\  & =7\\times 240 \\\\  & =1,680\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $140%$ of $1,200$ is $1,680.$ So, the weight of the second cake is $1,680\\,\\text{g}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527138 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527138 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527138 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527138 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(0,2)$  and $(0,5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527138 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23bd23d2-9b32-5ac7-a70a-faac16c61773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527138 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/f3/f329376e27ec08d2695db0e6d007e7a7b5342e7737c5d3f45eb524a2b1c2e572.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](images/66/66543bc73667ed70a965db4af2e657ad991660fc1b3a73a6e585943deffd3bbb.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/5d/5ded01b570e62d1e206aa8abd1012121367e60e749d441eb4e6ec7e3b12f6e8a.png)\n\nThe point $(0,5)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(0,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5-2=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527149 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527149 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527149 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527149 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $11\\,289\\,643$\n\nII. $13\\,298\\,784$\n\nIII. $215\\,153\\,238$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527149 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33724016-d9da-53c5-bcf3-d48acaf1fa22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527149 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$\n\n- The number $11\\,289\\,643$ is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- The number $13\\,298\\,784$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+3+2+9+8+7+8+4=42\n$$\n\nSo, $13\\,298\\,784$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $215\\,153\\,238$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n2+1+5+1+5+3+2+3+8=30\n$$\n\nSo, $215\\,153\\,238$ is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527150 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527150 #edb/ref 1069 "q-155548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527150 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527150 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/86/867e0d53894e1cacac86120e5c79b32071e2b0a4863b445e6d3492392a59461d.png)\n\nConsider the figure shown above. Which of the following triplets of points are collinear?\n\nI. $M,Q,J$\n\nII. $P,O,L$\n\nIII. $L,O,M$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527150 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34603e37-b60f-5eb1-996c-5382f05619c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527150 #edb/ref 1074 "- $M,Q,J$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/7f/7f707d8fbeb9853ccb9441bbdab3b0674c5f51da7f73da73d73ee6592dca04ea.png)\n\n- $P,O,L$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/02/028ae56c8a85699d4d6719df2f80a989f1271e392546d005093319af2365675d.png)\n\n \n\n- $L,O,M$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/bf/bf0579ecbc33ebe2d97dc1b305de0a89b0c22dadd6a11d0e88ac4a36acbd447f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527161 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527161 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527161 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the constant term in the expression $\\frac{2}{3}+6a-4b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527161 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "454efe3b-e281-5051-bb7a-cf90c5710624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527161 #edb/ref 1074 "The expression has three terms: $\\frac{2}{3}$, $6a$, and $-4b.$  The only term that does not contain any variables is $\\frac{2}{3}$, so $\\frac{2}{3}$ is the constant term." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527167 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527167 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527167 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527167 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of $12÷(-5),$ expressed as an **improper fraction** in lowest terms, is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527167 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61f14a3a-d997-54ee-80e7-e8241a88c524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527167 #edb/ref 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $(12)$ by a negative one $(-5).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n12÷(-5)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $5$ without any signs: $12÷5=\\frac{12}{5}.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n12÷(-5)=-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527169 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527169 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527169 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527169 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $\\frac{2}{3}-1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527169 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72a5b03b-3213-5354-ab4d-cb82e383745f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527169 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nSince both fractions have the same denominators, we can combine them into one fraction, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{2-4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $2-4$ using a number line:\n\n![](images/fd/fd7fc3fedd2dbf8c90592bbedfdc15181eae63e4d2cbd71a4c036b2d75e8921c.png)\n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{2-4}{3}=\\frac{-2}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527176 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527176 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527176 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527176 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3e/3e1675e22ef33aa40b96349c910f04178dd4b11b1e7e97f15a5314977eea914f.png)\n\nWhich vertices are adjacent to $F?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527176 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8670832a-c9ac-5ad5-acea-ff50047338a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527176 #edb/ref 1074 "$F$ is the endpoint of the sides $\\overset{―}{FG}$ and $\\overset{―}{EF}.$ Therefore, the vertices $E$ and $G$ are adjacent to $F.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527188 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527188 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527188 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527188 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527188 #edb/ref 1071 "$8(2u-5v)=${{field-1}}\n$u\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$v$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527188 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d8b1706-5dbe-56db-8638-95f308211cb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527188 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(2u-5v) & = \\\\ 8\\cdot 2u+8\\cdot (-5v) & = \\\\ (16u)+(-40v) & = \\\\ 16u-40v\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527191 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527191 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527191 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527191 #edb/ref 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing numbers in the following sentence?\n\nTo paint his car, Anthony mixes blue paint with thinner. ​He requires $\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons of paint for every gallon of thinner. Therefore, the mixture has a ratio of $\\boxed{}$ gallons of paint to $\\boxed{}$ gallons of thinner." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527191 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a55cec5a-6ff7-549a-82f5-76546ccf37c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527191 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's rewrite the fraction $\\frac{3}{4}$ as a ratio:\n\n$$\n3:4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mixture has a ratio of $3$ gallons of paint to $4$ gallons of thinner." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527207 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527207 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the like terms in the expression $10x-18y-7z+10?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c03bfbc1-ac68-5596-a4f4-f714ecc885a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527207 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the terms of the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(10x)+(-18y)+(-7z)+(10).\n$$\n\nNone of these terms have the same variable part. Consequently, there are no like terms in this expression." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527220 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527220 #edb/ref 1069 "q-186120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527220 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527220 #edb/ref 1071 "$1.2-5=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527220 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e12935f7-7208-5953-a547-9693833e1308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527220 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $5$ is larger than $1.2,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to carry out the subtraction in the reverse order:\n\n$$\n5-1.2=3.8\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.2-5=-3.8.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527232 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12252c44-f6ed-5a4c-877a-529ad585fdea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1970f46e-8dbe-50a6-9b8a-0d7228e09450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527237 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2059b1b0-20c9-5501-aea4-da8a1b553909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527237 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527239 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "225a8f92-e15a-59b5-bf0e-1b68e30dd8c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527241 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527241 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a01f71d-97d1-5207-b8b2-d2e65ddf34ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527241 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527242 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527242 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ca76ade-a0fe-5c1b-bf99-b23c6a48abbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527242 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921